<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Multi-targeted to match MCPBridge.Core itself (PRD §11). Reviewing this PR surfaced that the
         net8.0-windows leg ; the entire reason the multi-target exists ; had zero automated coverage:
         these tests only ever ran against the net10.0-windows build of Core. That is especially wrong
         for WireEnumNameConverter, which exists SPECIFICALLY because net8 lacks .NET 9+'s
         JsonStringEnumMemberNameAttribute, so the leg it was written for was the one leg never
         exercised. `dotnet test` now runs the whole suite once per TFM, which is what makes
         "byte-identical wire output on both legs" a claim CI can hold rather than one a live session
         confirmed once by hand. -->
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-windows;net8.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <!--
      The VM deliberately has exactly ONE .NET install (the .NET 10 SDK ; see the
      revit-connector-development skill for the multi-hour confusion that rule exists to prevent), and
      it cross-COMPILES the net8 leg happily. It cannot RUN a net8.0 test host, though, so without this
      `dotnet test` fails the net8 leg with "You must install or update .NET to run this application"
      ; which would leave the leg untested for a second reason after we just multi-targeted to fix the
      first. RollForward lets the net8-compiled assemblies execute on the installed .NET 10 runtime.

      FIDELITY CAVEAT, and it is sharper than "runtime differences are unlikely": under RollForward
      the net8-COMPILED assemblies execute against .NET 10's System.Text.Json ; and System.Text.Json
      is the single component whose net8/net10 difference is the entire reason WireEnumNameConverter
      exists. So WireEnumNameConverterTests "passing on both TFMs" is, at runtime, the same STJ twice.
      What these runs genuinely hold is the net8 COMPILATION (that the source compiles and links
      against net8's BCL at all, which is the half that actually broke before). They do NOT hold
      net8 RUNTIME behaviour, and no claim that they do should be made in a PR description.

      In production the net8 leg runs inside Revit 2025, which hosts .NET 8. CLOSING THIS GAP TAKES
      TWO STEPS, AND THE FIRST ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH ; an earlier version of this comment said it was,
      which would have left the gap open while looking closed:
        1. Install the .NET 8 *runtime* (Microsoft.NETCore.App + Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App 8.x).
           A runtime, NOT a second SDK ; that distinction is the whole point, see the skill's
           "exactly one SDK on the VM" rule, which stays intact.
        2. Drop RollForward below, or relax it to Disable/Minor. LatestMajor means "roll forward to
           the highest available major EVEN IF the requested one is present", so with it left in
           place the net8 assemblies keep executing on .NET 10 after step 1 ; same gap, now
           invisible because you would believe it was fixed.
      Then confirm empirically rather than assuming: `dotnet test` should report the net8 assembly
      running on an 8.x runtime. Until both steps are done, do not cite these runs as cross-runtime
      evidence.

      What DOES already evidence the net8 leg at runtime is live testing against Revit 2025 itself,
      which hosts .NET 8: auth (AuthRole), execute_script returning status "success"
      (ExecutionStatus) and discovery have all round-tripped through this converter there.
    -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>MCPBridge.Core.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\MCPBridge.Core\MCPBridge.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\MCPBridge.RevitAdapter\MCPBridge.RevitAdapter.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
